    "blogtextliquid_LBJgtB": {
      "type": "blogtextliquid",
      "custom_css": [],
      "name": "Blog Text Liquid",
      "settings": {
        "text_content": "<p>Coachella is 95 degrees, dusty, and entirely unreasonable. By hour three of Day One, most people smell like a combination of SPF 50, iced oat milk lattes, and dry earth. It is an extreme environment for fragrance, and most perfumes simply cannot survive it.</p><p>When the temperature hits triple digits, high heat acts like an accelerator pedal on perfume molecules. Heavy gourmands and suffocating winter ouds become cloying and overwhelming, while delicate fresh colognes evaporate into the desert air in under forty minutes.</p>"
      }
    },
